WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.046 --> 00:00:09.048
&gt;&gt;&gt; As of this morning, more

00:00:09.048 --> 00:00:11.984
than 11,000 TV and movie writers

00:00:12.117 --> 00:00:14.086
are on strike halting multiple

00:00:14.219 --> 00:00:15.721
productions and delaying new

00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:18.290
seasons of some of your favorite

00:00:18.424 --> 00:00:20.559
shows, and what this means is

00:00:20.692 --> 00:00:24.196
late night TV shows will see the

00:00:24.329 --> 00:00:25.697
first impact, because they rely

00:00:25.831 --> 00:00:35.207
on the writers for realtime

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:35.574
writing.

00:00:35.707 --> 00:00:37.543
And also with other shows like

00:00:37.676 --> 00:00:43.916
"Big brother" and "The boom"

00:00:44.049 --> 00:00:53.759
vaulted in the void.

00:00:53.892 --> 00:00:55.994
From Netflix to Disney plus,

00:00:56.128 --> 00:00:57.796
they have shows readily

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.265
available, and they can offer

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:02.000
new content for quite some time.

00:01:02.134 --> 00:01:04.336
As for the last strike in 2007,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.773
it left a mark, and cost roughly

00:01:07.906 --> 00:01:10.609
$2.1 billion after that strike

00:01:10.742 --> 00:01:12.144
lasted over 100 days.

00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:14.113
And right now, the media and

00:01:14.246 --> 00:01:15.647
tech companies are grappling

00:01:15.781 --> 00:01:17.449
with weak advertising market

00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:19.418
that has driven the stock prices

00:01:19.551 --> 00:01:21.186
down, and driven them to cut

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:24.123
cost and the labor forces.

00:01:24.256 --> 00:01:26.658
And the writer face a tough

00:01:26.792 --> 00:01:28.627
reality, too, because the tough

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.329
market and combined with lower

00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:33.198
pay is making the situation

00:01:33.332 --> 00:01:33.832
unsustainable.

00:01:33.966 --> 00:01:35.601
90% of the writers guild voted

00:01:35.734 --> 00:01:39.671
in favor of the strike just last

00:01:39.805 --> 00:01:40.205
year.

00:01:40.339 --> 00:01:40.506
Sara.

00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:41.607
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Kate.

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.144
The show runner of the show

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.747
"Adam ruins everything," Adam

00:01:49.148 --> 00:01:50.482
Conover, and since you are known

00:01:50.616 --> 00:01:51.950
for ruining everything, is this

00:01:52.084 --> 00:01:52.451
your a fault?

00:01:52.584 --> 00:01:54.720
&gt;&gt; Well, I am off of the clock,

00:01:55.154 --> 00:01:56.355
and I'm not ruining anything at

00:01:56.488 --> 00:01:57.890
the time being, except for maybe

00:01:58.023 --> 00:01:59.324
the profits of the companies.

00:01:59.458 --> 00:02:00.425
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:02:00.559 --> 00:02:02.194
Explain why you feel like it is

00:02:02.327 --> 00:02:04.730
so important that this strike

00:02:05.164 --> 00:02:07.666
goes forward and that it has to

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:08.200
happen now?

00:02:08.333 --> 00:02:10.602
&gt;&gt; So, the studios and the

00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:14.239
streamers have tried to turn

00:02:14.373 --> 00:02:24.316
television writering from a

00:02:24.449 --> 00:02:25.184
career into a gig job.

00:02:25.184 --> 00:02:26.084
They are spending more money to

00:02:26.218 --> 00:02:27.719
make the shows and making more

00:02:27.853 --> 00:02:29.188
profits than ever, but the

00:02:29.254 --> 00:02:30.355
writers are making less money,

00:02:30.489 --> 00:02:32.157
and employing us for less time,

00:02:32.291 --> 00:02:33.992
and if you are looking at the

00:02:34.126 --> 00:02:35.894
proposals that rejected that

00:02:36.028 --> 00:02:37.996
caused to us go on strike, it

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.232
would have been proposals to

00:02:40.365 --> 00:02:41.833
protect us and employed not just

00:02:41.967 --> 00:02:43.302
by the day or the weaker to

00:02:43.435 --> 00:02:45.637
month, but that we can build a

00:02:45.771 --> 00:02:48.874
sustainable life in New York or

00:02:49.208 --> 00:02:51.977
Los Angeles or where ever we

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:55.447
live and not to live in

00:02:55.581 --> 00:02:56.748
procarety, and we are fighting

00:02:56.882 --> 00:02:58.784
for a stable life like all

00:02:59.218 --> 00:02:59.284
workers.

00:02:59.284 --> 00:03:00.786
&gt;&gt; We are showing the sticking

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:01.253
points.

00:03:01.253 --> 00:03:03.222
Can you give us what some of the

00:03:03.222 --> 00:03:05.357
sticking points are, because 98%

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:13.232
of the writers were in favor of

00:03:13.332 --> 00:03:16.201
the strike, and what is it that

00:03:16.335 --> 00:03:21.340
you are not going to compromise

00:03:21.473 --> 00:03:21.607
on?

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.742
&gt;&gt; The writers room, and this is

00:03:23.875 --> 00:03:25.244
where the writers get together

00:03:25.244 --> 00:03:27.246
and break a story and write

00:03:27.246 --> 00:03:27.379
scripts.

00:03:27.512 --> 00:03:28.647
The companies are trying to

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.716
eliminate it, and make the room

00:03:30.849 --> 00:03:33.919
smaller, and employ us on a

00:03:34.052 --> 00:03:36.488
freelance basis and rather us

00:03:36.622 --> 00:03:39.791
stay home and email in the

00:03:39.925 --> 00:03:44.363
script and we cannot make a

00:03:44.496 --> 00:03:45.931
living that way.

00:03:46.064 --> 00:03:48.700
And we want a writer's room on

00:03:48.834 --> 00:03:50.068
every show, and that is a

00:03:50.202 --> 00:03:50.569
sticking point.

00:03:50.702 --> 00:03:52.471
We want to make sure that the

00:03:52.604 --> 00:03:53.705
writers are paid the same on

00:03:53.839 --> 00:03:54.906
streaming as television.

00:03:55.274 --> 00:03:58.043
My first show was for TRUTV and

00:03:58.176 --> 00:04:00.312
the second show was for Netflix,

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:04.182
and the second show had the same

00:04:04.316 --> 00:04:06.618
way, and on Netflix, no

00:04:06.752 --> 00:04:09.288
minimums, and residuals are less

00:04:09.288 --> 00:04:11.290
than 1% under broadcast, and

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:12.824
that is true for everybody in

00:04:12.958 --> 00:04:16.261
late night, and true for the

00:04:16.395 --> 00:04:17.396
screenwriters and true across

00:04:17.529 --> 00:04:19.698
all of the worker a yas.

00:04:19.831 --> 00:04:21.300
&gt;&gt; What about those who run the

00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:23.902
industry, and say, times are

00:04:24.036 --> 00:04:27.939
changing, and we are not making

00:04:28.073 --> 00:04:30.542
the money that we once did, ale

00:04:30.676 --> 00:04:32.577
though some of us argue that the

00:04:32.711 --> 00:04:33.679
shows are great.

00:04:33.812 --> 00:04:34.212
What would you say?

00:04:34.346 --> 00:04:41.386
&gt;&gt; I would point out that David

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.322
zazlov was paid $250 million

00:04:43.455 --> 00:04:46.024
last year, and a quarter of a

00:04:46.158 --> 00:04:47.693
billion, and that is about the

00:04:47.826 --> 00:04:49.628
same level of what 10,000

00:04:49.761 --> 00:04:51.330
writers are asking him to pay

00:04:51.396 --> 00:04:52.431
all of us collectively.

00:04:52.564 --> 00:04:54.366
So I would say that if you are

00:04:54.499 --> 00:05:00.772
paid $250 million and Ted

00:05:00.906 --> 00:05:04.543
sorantz made about $10 million

00:05:04.676 --> 00:05:07.946
last year, and it is ridiculous

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.683
to be paying that they cannot

00:05:11.817 --> 00:05:13.785
make it, because I know people

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:15.987
who have had to go on

00:05:16.121 --> 00:05:17.356
assistance, because they can't

00:05:17.456 --> 00:05:18.990
go on the year, and they have

00:05:19.358 --> 00:05:20.058
lost their homes.

00:05:20.192 --> 00:05:21.360
And it is the people who are

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.362
making shows for them that are

00:05:23.362 --> 00:05:24.563
making less.

00:05:24.696 --> 00:05:27.999
&gt;&gt; Adam Conover, you may have

